/*
 * EXAMPLE 7 — Queue<String>, singular case.
 *
 * Two different elements go in, so the run distinguishes first-in-first-out from
 * last-in-first-out: "first" must come back from dequeue and "second" must then
 * be at the front.  A queue that served the newest element first would satisfy
 * every clause about size and still fail `dequeuedElem == \old(head)`.
 *
 * The state variable that names the element at the front of the queue is called
 * `head` rather than `front`, so that the query function can keep the obvious
 * name front() without shadowing the field its own postcondition reads.
 */
test QueueDryRun {
    sequence: enqueue -> enqueue -> dequeue -> front -> isEmpty;

    inputs {
        enqueue[0].elem = "first";
        enqueue[1].elem = "second";
    }
}
